WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.920
I have two daughters.

2
00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.800
I know you have a son who's nine.

3
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.240
Yes.

4
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.590
We all sat and watched "The Willoughbys"

5
00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.990
and I loved it so much.

6
00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.753
You're the mom, Helga?

7
00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.530
Yes, who loves to knit?

8
00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.920
Probably more focused on her knitting than her mothering

9
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.920
which seems to be a trend in some of the roles I've played

10
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.020
but I assure you and everyone

11
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:30.880
that I'm not like that at home.

12
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.570 line:15%
I'm a very full hands-on mom. (laughing)

13
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.560 line:15%
Well, that was what was so interesting in the research.

14
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.690
You're such an involved mom, you love your son so much.

15
00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.410
You make decisions about your work,

16
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.360
you put him first, your friendships are based a lot

17
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.250
in the development of your child.

18
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.980
And yeah, "The Willoughbys" goes there, like "Vacation,"

19
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.790
they don't hold back.

20
00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:53.790
They don't hold back, but you know,

21
00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.370
my son actually loved the movie

22
00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.250
and I was a little unsure when we first watched it

23
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.470
and he thought it was hilarious.

24
00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.510
I think he totally gets the heightened level

25
00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.360
that my characters exist on.

26
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.020
And you could tell it's a movie that kids are gonna like

27
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.010
when my son watched it like four times through

28
00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.840
and he doesn't do that for everything I'm in.

29
00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.470
Like, some things he doesn't even want to come and watch

30
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.760
or doesn't really care but (chuckling)

31
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.171
which is probably right and appropriate.

32
00:01:21.171 --> 00:01:22.600
My kids do the same with me.

33
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.310
Some of the things I do, they really like,

34
00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.460
and some, it's not connecting yet.

35
00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:28.293
I mean, it's a really nice sign

36
00:01:28.293 --> 00:01:29.126
that he wanted to watch it

37
00:01:29.126 --> 00:01:30.540
a few days in a row over and over again

38
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.360
even when I wasn't home or in the room

39
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.800
which is a nice thing to hear.

40
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.970
It's so well done.

41
00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.110
It's on Netflix.

42
00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.380
It's Maya Rudolph, Ricky Gervais, you, Will Forte.

43
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.880
It's such an extraordinary cast

44
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.073
and it's a really powerful movie.

45
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.570
I love the message that it gives in the end

46
00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.960
that that love comes from the family you choose,

47
00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.630
not necessarily the family you were given.

48
00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.960
And I think that's a beautiful message

49
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.560
that we could use more than ever in our world.

50
00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.040
It feels like a little indie

51
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.740
of an animated film really, to me,

52
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.830
cause it's very different tonally

53
00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.060
than a lot of the big animated movies that you see.

54
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.350
But I think at the core, its message is so beautiful.

55
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.730
And I got to work with one of my comic heroes

56
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.540
very closely on this, which is Martin Short.

57
00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:15.420
He plays the father.

58
00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.430
And I feel like every moment that we shared in that movie

59
00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:22.900
was inspired by him and his comedy and his daringness,

60
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.090
exactly what you're talking about,

61
00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.670 line:15%
his willing to go all the way in his comedy

62
00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.570 line:15%
with every choice and every line.

63
00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.610
I think it's extremely elevated

64
00:02:32.708 --> 00:02:35.850
and all families, like, if they haven't already seen it,

65
00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.210
check it out, it's amazing.

66
00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:41.470
Now, you're doing a reboot of "Name That Tune"

67
00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.513
which is such an iconic show, on since 1952.

68
00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:51.570
And I love how you had a quote saying that Broadway

69
00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.100
was the heartbeat of New York City.

70
00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:58.480
And that you're also getting to feel that

71
00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.560
in the musicality of this show you're doing now.

72
00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:03.520
Absolutely.

73
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.610
It was one of the reasons I really wanted to do this.

74
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.260
At first, I thought it would just be a lot of fun

75
00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.170
and "Name That Tune" was a show that I watched growing up

76
00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.320
in my childhood home

77
00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.210
and would play along with my very musical family.

78
00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.110 line:15%
We'd all be yelling at the TV, trying to guess the song

79
00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:20.740 line:15%
in as few notes as we could against each other

80
00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.900 line:15%
as well as playing along with the contestants

81
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.450
which is something you can still do now.

82
00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.250
No answers were ever shown during our games

83
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.953
so it's a really fun one that you could play along with.

84
00:03:28.953 --> 00:03:33.953
But yeah, I was longing to be in a room

85
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:38.010
with live music and a live audience again,

86
00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.950
and to feel that buzz that I really only get

87
00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.050
from live experience

88
00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.990
and especially from the Broadway theater.

89
00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:49.990
And being New Yorkers, I have really come to feel

90
00:03:51.944 --> 00:03:54.950
what a part of New York we're missing

91
00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.694
by not having the Broadway lights on right now.

92
00:03:57.694 --> 00:04:00.520
I really do feel that Broadway

93
00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.170
not only brings obviously so much revenue to New York City

94
00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.950
but I do feel like it's part of the heartbeat of New York.

95
00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:11.090
And it's gonna be a little while till we're back

96
00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:13.490
singing and dancing and hoofing our taps

97
00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:15.630
on the stages of Broadway again.

98
00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.780
But I just sort of can't wait

99
00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.560
and my heart is really, at this moment, going out

100
00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.300
to the entire community that makes a Broadway show.

101
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.730
It is such a collaboration,

102
00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.320
every Broadway show that you work on,

103
00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.250
and there are hundreds of people,

104
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.757
artisans, creators, people that make the shoes, the hats,

105
00:04:33.757 --> 00:04:35.870
the choreography, the everything.

106
00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.780
I mean, obviously I think you all know all of this,

107
00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.330
but there are so many businesses

108
00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:40.560
that are losing their studios

109
00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.460
and artists that are really struggling right now

110
00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.000
because the Broadway community is not up and running

111
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.900
and they are part of the collaboration

112
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.120
that makes a Broadway show.

113
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.770
Well, hopefully we're gonna be back there soon

114
00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.320
and until then, you're gonna do this show.

115
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.987 line:15%
(upbeat music)

